EXT. SALT FLAT - ABANDONED STAGECOACH - HIGH NOON

White to the horizon. Heat shimmer bends the sky. A stagecoach tilts on a broken axle, one wheel cocked in the crust.

MARSHAL CULLEN BRODERICK, 50s, tin star, dust-gray duster, steps down from the coach, rifle across his arm, while behind him DUTCH, a lean outlaw in manacles, shuffles toward the shade, and LUPE VARGAS, 30s, the driver, slaps her hat against her knee, checks the horses, and mutters at the broken wheel, all three of them squinting at a dark speck on the horizon that keeps growing.

CULLEN
(to the others)
Nobody run. There's nowhere to run to.

DUTCH
(low, to Cullen)
Marshal, take these off me. If that's riders, I can shoot.

CULLEN
No.

LUPE
(to herself)
Three days. Three goddamn days of salt.

CULLEN
Lupe. Water.

She tosses him a canteen. He drinks, passes it to Dutch. Dutch drinks, passes it back. The speck becomes a rider.

DUTCH
That's a lone horse.

CULLEN
Could be a lone man.

LUPE
Could be a mirage.

The rider slows. Stops a hundred yards out. Raises a white cloth. Waves it.

Cullen lowers the rifle. Dutch exhales. Lupe pulls her hat down.
